{
  "id": "Compound::DB00224 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Indinavir, a protease inhibitor, is known to bind to Cyp3a7, a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics and certain hormones. This binding leads to the participation of Cyp3a7 in multiple metabolic pathways, including the metabolism of aromatase activity and oxidoreductase reactions involving molecular oxygen. These pathways ultimately contribute to the activation of Cyp2d6, suggesting a mechanistic link between Indinavir and Cyp2d6 through the indirect activation of the latter by Cyp3a7. The evidence from the three paths collectively supports a role for Cyp3a7 in the activation of Cyp2d6, with Indinavir acting as a modulator of this process. The consistent involvement of Cyp3a7 in the participation of Cyp2d6 across different pathways underscores the importance of this enzyme in the metabolic activation of Cyp2d6. The integrated explanation highlights the central role of Cyp3a7 in the metabolic activation of Cyp2d6, with Indinavir influencing this process through its interaction with Cyp3a7.",
